-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1 — $(f+g+h)(x^k_g) - (f+g+h)(x^*) = o(1/\sqrt{k+1})$
-- statement:
--   In the setting of problem (3.1) ($f, g : H \to (-\infty, +\infty]$ closed, proper, convex; $h : H \to \mathbb R$ convex, differentiable, $\nabla h$ $\beta^{-1}$-Lipschitz, $\beta > 0$), let $\gamma \in (0, 2\beta)$ and let $(z^k, x^k_g, x^k_f)_{k \ge 0}$ be generated by Algorithm 2 with $\lambda_k \equiv 1$ from $z^0 \in H$. Let $z^*$ be a fixed point of $T$, $x^* = \operatorname{prox}_{\gamma g}(z^*)$, and suppose that $f$ is finite and $L$-Lipschitz continuous on the closed ball $B\big(x^*, (1 + \gamma/\beta)\|z^0 - z^*\|\big)$. Then $(f+g+h)(x^*)$ and every $(f+g+h)(x^k_g)$ are finite, and
--   $$(f+g+h)(x^k_g) - (f+g+h)(x^*) = o\Big(\frac{1}{\sqrt{k+1}}\Big),\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \sqrt{k+1}\,\Big((f+g+h)(x^k_g) - (f+g+h)(x^*)\Big) \to 0 .$$
--
--   This is the non-ergodic (last-iterate) objective rate of the three-operator splitting scheme for problem (3.1).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper prints the ball as $B(0, (1+\gamma/\beta)\|z^0 - z^*\|)$; its proof applies Lemma 3.1, whose ball is centred at $x^* = J_{\gamma B}(z^*)$, so the centre $0$ is a typo and the ball is centred at $x^*$ here. The Lipschitz hypothesis is stated for the real-valued restriction of $f$ together with finiteness of $f$ on the ball, which is what "$f$ is $L$-Lipschitz on the ball" means for an extended-valued $f$. The objective values are extended reals; their finiteness is part of the conclusion, and the gap is then taken between real numbers.
-- source:
--   Davis and Yin, A Three-Operator Splitting Scheme and its Optimization Applications, Set-Valued Var. Anal. 25 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1007/s11228-017-0421-z, p. 838, Theorem 3.1

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_ConvexRates_Problem
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_ConvexRates_Algorithm

open InnerProductSpace Filter Topology

namespace ThreeOpSplitting.ConvexRates

/-- Theorem 3.1: if `f` is `L`-Lipschitz on the closed ball `B(x*, (1 + γ/β)‖z⁰ - z*‖)`,
`x* = prox_{γg}(z*)`, then `(f+g+h)(x^k_g) - (f+g+h)(x*) = o(1/√(k+1))`: the objective values are
finite and `√(k+1) ((f+g+h)(x^k_g) - (f+g+h)(x*)) → 0`. -/
theorem last_iterate_rate
    {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H] [CompleteSpace H]
    (f g : H → EReal) (h : H → ℝ) (β γ : ℝ) (hβ : 0 < β) (hγ : 0 < γ) (hγβ : γ < 2 * β)
    (hf : IsProperClosedConvex f) (hg : IsProperClosedConvex g) (hh : IsSmoothConvex β h)
    (proxf proxg : H → H) (hproxf : IsProx γ f proxf) (hproxg : IsProx γ g proxg)
    (z0 zstar : H) (hfix : splittingOp proxf proxg (gradient h) γ zstar = zstar)
    (L : NNReal)
    (hfin : ∀ x ∈ Metric.closedBall (proxg zstar) ((1 + γ / β) * ‖z0 - zstar‖), f x ≠ ⊤)
    (hLip : LipschitzOnWith L (fun x => (f x).toReal)
      (Metric.closedBall (proxg zstar) ((1 + γ / β) * ‖z0 - zstar‖))) :
    objective f g h (proxg zstar) ≠ ⊤ ∧
      (∀ k : ℕ, objective f g h (algXg proxf proxg (gradient h) γ z0 k) ≠ ⊤) ∧
      Tendsto (fun k : ℕ => Real.sqrt ((k : ℝ) + 1) *
          ((objective f g h (algXg proxf proxg (gradient h) γ z0 k)).toReal - (objective f g h (proxg zstar)).toReal))
        atTop (𝓝 0) := by sorry

end ThreeOpSplitting.ConvexRates
